Object.assign(EXPLANATIONS,{
 ato:{title:'Assemble-to-Order and the module stock point',body:`<p>For the Canon reference, common modules are built and stocked before a customer order. The finished printer is assembled after that order. This places the <b>customer-order decoupling point</b> between module stock and final assembly.</p><p><b>ATO and CTO can coexist:</b> ATO describes fulfilment and the stock point; CTO describes selecting a valid customer configuration. Configuring a printer does not automatically mean engineering a new product.</p><p>The ATO walkthrough illustrates <b>linked assembly processing</b>: the sales item creates assembly supply. Strategy 82 is a production-order example; 81 uses a planned order. Static processing means a 1:1 link; dynamic processing permits a looser relationship. These words do not mean that every availability check includes finite capacity.</p><p>The CTO walkthrough keeps the same common-module stock point but illustrates order-driven production planning instead of that direct link. The underlay therefore compares chosen reference routes, not universal rules attached to their names.</p><p>Confirm the requirements class, stock segmentation, module consumption, configuration engine and target-release support for Canon. Common components can remain collective in MTO too.</p>`,sources:['atoSettings','atoVc']},
 'ato-check':{title:'Assembly availability and customer confirmation',body:`<p>Assembly processing can create assembly supply during sales-order processing and check selected components. The documented classic configurable-product route checks <b>top-level components</b>; it must not be presented as an automatic full multilevel or finite-capacity check.</p><p>In this reference, the module check identifies the covered quantity and any shortage. PP/DS then validates relevant material and assembly-resource dates in its agreed scope. The result supports the customer date through the configured confirmation integration.</p><p>When modules are missing, plan their replenishment and its timing. A reservation or planned receipt is different from a physical issue or usable receipt. An immediate positive confirmation is not guaranteed.</p><p>aATP/SBC is a separate supported-route choice to validate for the edition, release and configured product. PAL, BOP and ABC remain optional. Strategy 81/82 by itself does not prove compatibility with every AVC/PP/DS/aATP combination.</p>`,sources:['atoCheck','atoSettings','atoVc','sbcFeatures']},
 mix:{title:'Printer forecast, attachment rates and configuration',body:`<p>Forecast the printer family, then translate it into expected module usage. For ten printers, a 40% share of the 7-head option means four 7-head and six 5-head print modules. A 70% share changes those expectations to seven and three, while printer demand remains ten.</p><p>History helps establish the mix; planners can adjust it for a trend or commercial information. The configurator still selects the actual valid combination for the customer order. Rates do not validate that configuration.</p><p>Model the split in the agreed IBP planning products, attributes, key figures and structures. A representative planning product is an example choice, not proof that the full KMAT rules execute in IBP.</p><p><b>Product terminology:</b> the source document’s CBF reference belongs to SAP APO. CU80 is classic configuration reporting, not an IBP forecasting transaction. Modern IBP characteristics-based planning has its own attribute mapping and setup; it is distinct from blindly importing the live AVC rule engine.</p>`,sources:['cbf','cu80','cbp','demandScope']},
 inventory:{title:'Buffer policy, uncertainty and inventory cost',body:`<p>A module buffer separates replenishment from final customer assembly. Its target should reflect forecast error, supply and lead-time uncertainty, and the service objective. Supplier on-time performance helps reveal replenishment risk.</p><p>IBP inventory optimization can recommend inventory targets across locations and levels. Planners and the business review the cost and service consequences. More buffer is an investment; a service target is not a guarantee for every order.</p><p>In the explorer, <b>extra units per module type</b> is a manual policy example. It increases planned and then received module stock, while the demand forecast remains ten printers. It is not an inventory-optimization result or a computed service percentage.</p><p>Safety time is a separate timing policy to validate; it is not automatically the same output as an IBP safety-stock recommendation. Demand-driven replenishment is another buffer design with distinct signals and setup, not a compulsory extension of this forecast/PIR route.</p>`,sources:['inventory','inventoryScope','ddr']},
 'ibp-deep':{title:'IBP planning responsibilities for the Canon reference',body:`<p>The planning loop starts with history and business assumptions. Demand planning agrees volume; attachment rates represent the expected module mix. S&OP reviews supply, capacity and the inventory/service trade-off. An agreed stock policy then informs the selected supply-planning route.</p><p>With <b>S/4-owned supply</b>, the chosen forecast demand is integrated as S/4 PIRs and stock-policy settings are kept distinct. With <b>IBP order-based supply</b>, accepted supply proposals are handed over under agreed ownership. Execution and fulfilment feed subsequent planning reviews.</p><p>Inventory optimization, demand sensing, response planning and demand-driven replenishment are capabilities to assess against the business need. They are not a mandatory minimum software stack for Canon.</p><p>Planning horizons should cover the actual lead times and business decisions. MRP has no universal 16-week limit. Current ECC LIS/SOP, BW and Excel use comes from the discussion; confirm the specific replacement and migration scope for the target S/4 edition.</p><p>The current example does not run an optimizer, predict a service percentage, or calculate an assembly lead time.</p>`,sources:['demandScope','supply','inventory','plannedOrders','ownership']}
});
EXPLANATIONS.assumptions.body=`<p>The opening view focuses on the <b>ATO hypothesis for Canon</b>: modules stocked anonymously, finished printers assembled after the customer order. It is a reference to validate, not confirmation of Canon’s final system design.</p><p>ATO, MTS, MTO and CTO use the same illustrative order and agreed planning design. ATO and CTO can overlap. Extra-stock settings are manual examples; no optimizer, real lead-time calculation or guaranteed service level is implied.</p>`+EXPLANATIONS.assumptions.body;
EXPLANATIONS.strategies.body=EXPLANATIONS.strategies.body.replace('<p><b>82 · Assembly processing.</b>', '<p><b>81 / 82 · Assembly processing.</b>').replace('A possible direct link from sales to an assembly production order.', '81 uses planned-order assembly supply; 82 is a production-order reference. The exact assembly type controls the link.');
Object.assign(TERMS,{'ATO':'Assemble-to-Order: stock common modules before demand, then assemble the finished product after the customer order. It can also use customer configuration (CTO).','Customer-order decoupling point':'The point where forecast-driven supply meets customer-driven fulfilment. Here ATO holds common modules at that point, while MTS holds finished printers.','Assembly order':'Assembly supply linked to a sales item under the selected assembly-processing setup. The reference uses a 1:1 production-order link; other assembly types differ.','Safety stock':'Extra inventory positioned to buffer uncertainty. It is a stock target distinct from forecast demand and from actual on-hand inventory.','S&OP':'Sales and Operations Planning: agree demand, supply and capacity choices together with service, inventory and financial implications.','Forecast error':'The difference between a forecast made earlier and the demand realized later, at the relevant planning level and lag. Forecast consumption is a different calculation.','Lead-time variability':'How actual replenishment times vary around their expectation; it matters for buffer policy and reliable customer commitments.','CBF / CBP':'APO Characteristics-Based Forecasting and IBP characteristics-based planning are distinct product capabilities. The simple attachment-rate example should not be labelled an IBP CU80 process.'});
ROLES.splice(4,0,['ibp-inventory','Inventory policy / SAP IBP','PackageCheck','Size and position buffers','Forecast error, lead-time variability, service and cost assumptions','Reviewed inventory targets / buffer policy','Supply planning and operational stock-policy settings','Inventory optimization is an optional method for recommending targets; the illustrative extra units are a manual choice. Supplier delivery performance and actual replenishment times help explain risk. Safety stock is not additional customer forecast.']);
function renderIbp(){
 const m=getModulePlan({...state,scenario:'ato'}),cards=[['history','Inputs','Order history, shipments, commercial signals','ChartNoAxesCombined'],['forecast','Demand planning','Agree printer/model volume and horizon','ChartNoAxesCombined'],['mix','Module mix','Expected attachment rates and trends','SlidersHorizontal'],['balance','S&OP','Supply, capacity, service and investment','Network'],['buffer','Inventory policy','Stock point, forecast error and lead times','PackageCheck'],['handover','Supply handover','S/4 requirements or IBP supply proposals','Network'],['review','Execution feedback','Actual quantities, mix and supplier delivery','Truck']];
 const explainKeys={history:'ibp-deep',forecast:'ibp-deep',mix:'mix',balance:'ibp-deep',buffer:'inventory',handover:'supply',review:'ibp-deep'};
 $('#ibp-planning').innerHTML=`<div class="lesson-banner"><span>${icon('Layers3')}</span><div><b>Plan the module buffer before the printer order</b><p>The reference puts common modules at the customer-order decoupling point. IBP planning decisions determine the expected mix and stock policy; S/4 produces, assembles and delivers.</p></div></div><div class="ibp-flow panel"><div class="section-row"><h3>Planning decisions and feedback</h3><button class="text-link" data-explain="ibp-deep">Read the planning loop ↗</button></div><div class="ibp-flow-grid">${cards.map(([id,t,d,ic],i)=>`<button class="ibp-flow-card" data-explain="${explainKeys[id]}"><span class="ibp-flow-number">0${i+1}</span>${icon(ic)}<b>${t}</b><small>${d}</small><span class="ibp-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></button>`).join('')}</div><p class="small muted">These are responsibilities and decisions, not a list of mandatory software modules or jobs. The next planning cycle uses the feedback; an individual order does not automatically rewrite the statistical forecast.</p></div><div class="panel ibp-numbers"><div class="section-row"><div><p class="eyebrow">SAME 10-PRINTER FORECAST</p><h3>Change the mix or the stock policy</h3></div><button class="text-link" data-explain="inventory">What determines the buffer? ↗</button></div><div class="module-controls"><label>7-head attachment rate <select id="ibp-mix"><option value="10">10%</option><option value="40">40% baseline</option><option value="70">70% trend</option><option value="90">90%</option></select></label><label>Extra units / module type <select id="ibp-buffer"><option value="0">0 units</option><option value="1">1 unit</option><option value="2">2 units</option></select></label></div><div class="ibp-numeric-flow"><div><span>PRINTER FORECAST</span><b>10</b><small>Model units; unchanged</small></div><span>→</span><div><span>EXPECTED PRINT MODULES</span><b>${m.five} / ${m.seven}</b><small>5-head / 7-head</small></div><span>+</span><div><span>MANUAL BUFFER EXAMPLE</span><b>${m.buffer} each</b><small>Additional stock-target units</small></div><span>→</span><div class="focus"><span>PREBUILD TARGET</span><b>${m.stockFive} / ${m.stockSeven}</b><small>Not physical stock yet</small></div></div><p>Feeder and finishing targets are ${m.feeder} each. This changes future supply and inventory investment, while demand remains 10 printers. No service percentage, lead time or inventory optimum is calculated.</p><button class="button secondary" data-explore="ato">Follow this ATO plan into execution ${icon('ArrowRight')}</button></div><div class="split-panels"><div class="panel"><h3>Choose the operational handover</h3><p><b>IBP demand & buffers → S/4 supply:</b> integrate the intended forecast as PIRs; transfer agreed stock-policy settings separately. MRP/PP/DS plans the operational supply.</p><p><b>IBP order-based supply → S/4 execution:</b> hand accepted supply proposals to S/4 with agreed material/horizon ownership. The walkthrough leaves additional PIR transfer out of this route.</p><button class="text-link" data-explain="supply">Integration and ownership details ↗</button></div><div class="panel violet-panel"><h3>Capabilities to assess against the business need</h3><p>Demand sensing can support short-term signals. Inventory optimization can recommend stock targets. Response planning/allocation, collaboration and demand-driven replenishment are further design choices.</p><p>Start with the specific service, shortage, planning-effort and inventory problem. Compare the value and complexity of each option; none is automatically a required extra component.</p><button class="text-link" data-explain="inventory">Inventory optimization and DDR ↗</button></div></div><details class="sources-block"><summary>Product terminology and assumptions to validate</summary><p>The source’s CU80/CBF example should not be used as an IBP transaction. CBF in SAP APO and modern IBP characteristics-based planning are distinct. The attachment-rate design requires an agreed mapping; it does not run all live AVC rules automatically.</p><p>MRP has no fixed 16-week horizon. Confirm actual lead times, tactical horizon, the target S/4 edition and the replacement scope for ECC LIS/SOP, BW and Excel. Safety-time policy is distinct from an IBP safety-stock recommendation.</p>${sourceLinks(['cbf','cu80','cbp','demandScope','inventory'])}</details>`;
 $('#ibp-mix').value=state.attach;$('#ibp-buffer').value=state.buffer;
 $('#ibp-mix').onchange=e=>{stop();state.attach=Number(e.target.value);state.index=0;renderJourney();renderIbp();};$('#ibp-buffer').onchange=e=>{stop();state.buffer=Number(e.target.value);state.index=0;renderJourney();renderIbp();};
}

EXPLANATIONS.assumptions.body=EXPLANATIONS.assumptions.body.replace('The CTO print-module mix is 60% 5-head and 40% 7-head.', 'The opening module mix is 60% 5-head and 40% 7-head; the controls can change it.').replace('No other orders, stock commitments, scrap, safety stock or lot-size constraints are included.', 'No other orders, stock commitments, scrap or lot-size constraints are included. Extra module stock is included only when selected in the manual buffer example.');
EXPLANATIONS.supply.body=EXPLANATIONS.supply.body.replace('<b>CTO</b>', '<b>ATO / CTO</b>').replace('adds network supply planning and what-if scenarios.', 'adds network supply planning and what-if scenarios. The S/4 supply route also retains the upstream demand, S&OP and buffer decisions.').replace('IBP supplies the demand outlook, while S/4 plans operational supply.', 'IBP supplies the agreed demand and stock-policy decisions, while S/4 plans operational supply.');
